The Corruption Behind Animal Testing 2 
Abstract 
Animal testing has been a social discussion for the last few decades, we as a society do not  seem to care about animal testing beyond the point of discussion. This paper will discuss why we  use animal testing, the possibility of how we can replace animal testing, and why haven’t we replaced animal testing already. Towards the end of this paper, I hope readers can come to the  same conclusion as me, which involves a reform of the current governing system, and hope that by doing so, we can save the animals and pave a better path for the future of science. Keywords: Animal Testing, corruption
The Corruption Behind Animal Testing 3 
Animal testing regulatory body needs a reform due to corruptions 
In the modern world, our pharmaceuticals have achieved a level we have never seen  before. with a great leap of science always came at a cost, which is animal testing. Animal  testing is a cornerstone of modern medicine, there's no denying it, but with new technology  emerging, animal testing is becoming less and less necessary. But companies are abusing this  system, making products for consumers, for profit, but not for the science or so they claim.  Companies who are profiting off these tests are making billions and billions, they pay the  regulators to keep the companies unchecked. And no one is checking on their procedures, no one  is making sure these animals are safe. This is important. checking on animals and making sure  these companies are following the laws. the regulatory body is so small and underfunded, that  about 100 people are to check around 9,000 facilities, of those 9,000 facilities, 1,200 of them are  animal experimental labs, within each of these facilities, there could be thousands of animals  need to be inspected. (PETA, 2020), the agents who are supposed to be checking these animal  tests are not familiar with chemistry and animals, and they can't possibly decide what is needed  or what's important for science or can't even be checked thoroughly. These tests should be  thought out, between two tests there needs to be improvements, Neurlink used 1,500 animals  over 4 years, which is unacceptable. There are no improvements in animal testing, animals are  not just a tool, they are animals with intelligence. 
Regional animal testing laws are often useless 
There is no denying that regional animal testing laws are created with animal welfare in  mind, but they have been proven to be ineffective and full of limitations. Each region works on  its own with its own culture and standards in mind, this creates a patchwork of policies that lacks consistency and transparency. This kind of law only creates confusion for the companies that
