operate at an international level and also create loopholes that can be exploited by those who have  questioned standard companies. 
The variability in regional laws encourages companies to engage in “regulatory  arbitrage”. This leads to companies strategically conducting these tests in regions with little to no  regulation and enforcement, thus evading ethical oversight. For instance, “a pharmaceutical  company will choose to conduct its animal testing in a country where animal testing is  mandatory such as China” (Fischer, K. 2015). By doing so, these billion-dollar companies will  save millions on costs, while making billions from regions that disapprove of animal testing.  While it does not break any regional laws, these companies will use the research they gained  from China and sell the products to regions with strict animal testing laws, such as Europe. 
Additionally, the limited scope of regional laws, these laws typically focus on one  specific type of testing, such as cosmetics. In some cases, these narrowed-down animal testing  regulations will often overlook the importance of animal testing. They often overlook the long term effects of drugs and opt for an all-around no-animal-testing policy without further  investigation. 
These regional animal tests may be ethically well-intentioned. Still, their ability to protect  and limit unethical animal testing is often nonexistent and adds to the limitation on testing that  requires animal testing. 
Animal testing has become obsolete in a lot of cases 
Animal Testing has been seen as a “necessary evil” in scientific and medical research,  however, with AI and technology advancing, animal testing now has many more human and  sometimes more accurate alternatives.
The Corruption Behind Animal Testing 5 
Development of nonanimal model alternative methods, such as “Testing cells and tissues  in test tubes or cell cultures, 3D tissue culture, Computational and mathematical models, Stem  cell research, no-invasive diagnostic imaging, and clinical research” (NIH,2022). These  alternatives not only provide more accurate test results but can also bypass species differences  that can sometimes result in animal testing outcomes being inconclusive or misleading. 
Many drugs that show promising results in animal testing have many times failed in human clinical trials. “This failure of animal test results to translate to humans is at least  partially responsible for the lack of safe, effective, and affordable medicines for those who need  them. 92% of potential drugs currently fail in human clinical trials, mostly because they prove unsafe or ineffective in humans.” (Cruelty-Free International, 2023). 
In many cases, even after animal testing and clinical trials, drugs still can be proven to  have safety risks which result in a “Black Box” (black box is warming that adverse drug  reactions could cause death or serious injury), for example, “Adverse drug reactions (ADRs) are  now a leading cause of death, killing 197,000 people in the EU each year (European  Commission, 2008), and over 125,000 in the us (Light, 2015).” (Archibald, K., Coleman, R., &  Drake, T. 2019). 
The current regulatory system isn’t working as intended. 
The regulatory body for animal testing has been a double-edged sword, while it appears  to be designed to ensure ethical treatment for all animals, it inadvertently limited the scholars to  do research on important issues. This regulation imposed on animal testing often creates a  bureaucratic maze for already underfunded scholars to navigate, this often results in delays,  increased costs, and sometimes, abandonment of projects that have groundbreaking potential.
The Corruption Behind Animal Testing 6 
The ethical concerns are valid, but the bureaucratic hurdles are holding science from advancing  to a new level. 
With every outcry from the public, or rather the election cycle, policymakers will create new rules out of thin air and create more red tape for scholars to jump through, but these rules  never seem to bother big cosmetic companies from making profits. Of the 50 largest cosmetics  companies, about 88% of them are funding animal testing, and of those 50 companies, 44 of  them are not cruelty-free. Some of the biggest brands include NARS, L’Oreal, Estee Lauder, and Lancôme. All these are multi-billion companies that are getting all these green lights on animal  testing. 
While the scholars are suffering from all the red tape, these companies are making  billions off the products they tested on animals. These beauty product companies pour money  into regulatory bodies, to make sure animal testing and overseeing agencies are giving them all  the leeway they could have. 
The nature of regulatory standards has created a situation where cosmetic companies to  keep testing on animals if they wish, but scholars are been limited to a set of rules for their new  rules. (Archibald, K., Coleman, R., & Drake, T. 2019) 
Idea to reform 
It is depressing to see that, in this society of rampant corruption, even the authorities in  charge of animal testing have fallen under the same bureaucracy. Some regulatory agencies'  deteriorating ethical standards have had a cascading effect, entwining other legislation in a state of nepotism and bureaucracy. The shortcomings of elected governments become evident when  they try to implement "no animal testing" legislation. Due to the worldwide nature of industries, large corporations can easily shift their animal testing operations to another location if limits are  imposed in one area thanks to loopholes in international law.
The Corruption Behind Animal Testing 7 A global collaborative effort might be the way out of this moral maze. Perhaps the necessary revolutionary step would be an agency, modeled like the Geneva Convention, uniting  states against the inhumane treatment of animals. The concept is to demolish current animal  experimentation facilities and create a strong global organization, something like 'Interpol for  Animal Testing,' with the power to investigate, capture, and punish violators. An organization like this might ensure worldwide accountability by prosecuting offenders. 
Recognizing that systemic change requires a holistic approach, attention must also be  focused on the decision-makers who perpetuate these difficulties. The call to action calls for a  complete revamping of political systems. The removal of current politicians and decision-makers,  who are frequently involved in backdoor agreements, is critical. A revamped election system that  prioritizes the popular vote ensures that those who care about ethical concerns and public interests 
get elected. Term limits prohibit politicians from remaining in power for long periods, lowering  the chance of back-door deals and developing a responsive political landscape. It is critical to break away from the restraints of long-lasting political parties, as  demonstrated by senators who have been out of touch with the public's changing needs and wishes  for decades. The proposed reform aims to put people in positions of authority who are accountable,  responsive, and committed to addressing ethical challenges, such as the widespread practice of  unnecessary animal testing. This holistic approach, which includes both global treaties and political reform, is proposed as a feasible remedy for breaking the cycle of corruption and ensuring a future in which animal welfare is protected without compromise. It may be the only way to chart a course toward a more ethical and accountable world. 
Regulating animal testing might cause the economy to crash 
While animal tasting is still up for debate, some people might argue that with animal tasting, we as a  society are making great money from it. Particularly in the pharmaceutical and biomedical industries, these
